@endnode

@node firmware-updates-zzfwupdate "Firmware updates - ZZFwUpdate"
@{b}ZZFwUpdate@{ub} copies a firmware image from AmigaOS to the ZZ9000's FAT32
microSD card without removing the card. The usual update flow:

ZZFwUpdate RAM:BOOT.bin

Power-cycle the Amiga after replacing @{b}BOOT.bin@{ub} so the ZZ9000 boots the new
firmware. The ZZTop Project menu offers the same operation with a file
requester.

By default the destination filename on the SD card is the source basename.
To write a different root-level filename, pass it as the optional second
argument:

ZZFwUpdate SYS:Storage/zz9000-fw.bin BOOT.bin

The destination name must be 1-64 characters from @{b}A-Z@{ub}, @{b}a-z@{ub}, @{b}0-9@{ub}, @{b}.@{ub}, @{b}_@{ub}, or
@{b}-@{ub}.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node backups-and-rollback "Backups and rollback"
When you replace @{b}BOOT.bin@{ub}, the firmware keeps the previous image as
@{b}BOOT.bak@{ub}. If a new firmware boots but misbehaves, roll back without
removing the card:

ZZFwUpdate RESTORE

This promotes @{b}BOOT.bak@{ub} to the active @{b}BOOT.bin@{ub} (discarding the replaced
image, so no backup remains afterwards) after a confirmation prompt. Pass
@{b}-y@{ub} to skip the prompt, or a name to restore something other than @{b}BOOT.bin@{ub}.
Restore talks to the @{i}running@{ui} firmware, so it recovers a
booting-but-misbehaving update; a card that does not boot at all still
needs the microSD removed and restored on another computer. RESTORE needs
firmware with FWUP cmd 5 support.

@{"Back" link firmware-updates-zzfwupdate}

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node networking-zz9000net-device "Networking - ZZ9000Net.device"
@{b}ZZ9000Net.device@{ub} in @{b}Devs:Networks/@{ub} is the SANA-II Ethernet driver. It
programs the firmware's multicast hash filter, so IPv6 and
service-discovery stacks receive their groups. It adopts the MAC address
from @{b}ZZ9000.CFG@{ub} (@{b}mac@{ub}), honours @{b}int2 = on@{ub}, and works with any SANA-II stack
(Roadshow, Miami DX, Genesis).

The installer can also install a Roadshow NetInterface template
(@{b}Devs:NetInterfaces/ZZ9000@{ub}) configured for DHCP. When the installer asks
about network setup, answering yes writes the template; Roadshow then
brings the interface up with no manual @{b}NetConfig@{ub} editing.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node zznetstats "ZZNetStats"
@{b}ZZNetStats@{ub} opens @{b}ZZ9000Net.device@{ub}, requests SANA-II global stats, and
prints the firmware RX queue, backpressure and drop counters:

ZZNetStats
ZZNetStats DEVICE=Networks/ZZ9000Net.device UNIT=0
ZZNetStats Networks/ZZ9000Net.device 0

Run it before and after a throughput test to see whether drops happen in
the Amiga-side driver or in the firmware RX path.

@{"Back" link networking-zz9000net-device}

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node board-diagnostics-zzdiag "Board diagnostics - ZZDiag"
@{b}ZZDiag@{ub} dumps the useful hardware-facing diagnostics in one place:

ZZDiag
ZZDiag 3 50

The optional arguments are sample count and AmigaDOS delay ticks between
samples. It reports the board identity, firmware version, VideoCap state,
USB, SD, AX/audio and Ethernet diagnostic registers - the VideoCap section
includes the detailed capture and genlock registers when the running
firmware exposes them. It also checks whether the installed firmware,
drivers and SDK payloads are a matched set; include its output when
reporting a problem.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node boot-time-link-check-zznetready "Boot-time link check - ZZNetReady"
@{b}ZZNetReady@{ub} answers one question for @{b}S:User-Startup@{ub}: is the ZZ9000 present,
and is its Ethernet port up? It needs no TCP/IP stack:

ZZNetReady
ZZNetReady TIMEOUT=10 QUIET

Exit codes: @{b}0@{ub} link ready (also returned on firmware that cannot report link
state, so the old always-start behaviour is kept), @{b}5@{ub} not ready - no card,
no link within @{b}TIMEOUT@{ub} seconds (default 5), or unreadable arguments.
Everything except OK returns WARN because AmigaDOS shells run
@{b}S:User-Startup@{ub} with FAILAT 10 by default: a failure code would abort the
rest of the script before @{b}If WARN@{ub} can branch. A typical @{b}S:User-Startup@{ub}
gating:

ZZNetReady QUIET
If WARN
  ; no card or no link - skip the stack this boot
Else
  Run SYS:Network/... ; or your stack's start script
EndIf

Requires firmware with @{b}ETH_CONFIG_CAP_LINK_STATE@{ub} for real link reporting
(v2.8.1); the bit means negotiation completed since power-on, not that the
cable is in right now.

@{b}ZZCapture@{ub} extends native-video diagnosis with row timing and C28
calibration evidence. Its phase-changing commands are experimental; see
@{b}ZZCapture.guide@{ub} before using them.

@{"Back" link board-diagnostics-zzdiag}

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node scanlines-zzscanlines "Scanlines - ZZScanlines"
@{b}ZZScanlines@{ub} controls the scanline modes of the scandoubler output:

ZZScanlines 0
ZZScanlines 1 0
ZZScanlines 2 1
ZZScanlines 3 0

Modes are @{b}0=off@{ub}, @{b}1=classic@{ub}, @{b}2=soft@{ub}, @{b}3=gradient@{ub}; parity is @{b}0=odd dark@{ub},
@{b}1=even dark@{ub}.

Like ZZTop's Scandoubler window, @{b}ZZScanlines@{ub} changes the live FPGA state
immediately. To make scanlines survive a power cycle, save them to
@{b}ZZ9000.CFG@{ub} - the ZZTop Scandoubler window's Save button does that.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
